    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e${{cost_ps}} a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost). The dried chiles and the turkey breast are the two real costs, and the chiles keep for a year in a sealed bag, so the next pot is cheaper than this one.",
    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eBuy dried chiles from the Hispanic aisle, not the spice rack.\u003c/strong\u003e The big bags of ancho and guajillo cost a fraction of what the little branded packets do, and they keep for a year sealed in the pantry.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCanned hominy is the cheap route.\u003c/strong\u003e Dried hominy needs an overnight soak and hours of cooking. Canned is drained, rinsed and ready, and it costs about the same per serving.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eTurkey breast is a holiday buy.\u003c/strong\u003e Grab a frozen roast when the seasonal price hits, cook and shred it, and freeze it in portions. Pozole is exactly what that freezer stash is for.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eKeep the garnishes separate.\u003c/strong\u003e The shredded cabbage, cilantro and lime go into their own small containers. Add them at the table so they stay crisp instead of going limp in the broth."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Stem and seed the dried ancho and guajillo chiles, toast them in a dry skillet for about 30 seconds a side until they smell nutty, then cover them in hot water and soak 20 minutes.",
                    "Blend the softened chiles with some of their soaking water, a chunk of the onion and several cloves of garlic into a smooth red sauce, then strain it.",
                    "Heat the olive oil in your big pot, cook the rest of the diced onion and garlic until soft, and fry the strained chile sauce in the hot oil for five minutes so it darkens and loses its raw edge.",
                    "Pour in the chicken broth along with the dried oregano, ground cumin, bay leaves, salt and black pepper, then simmer 30 minutes.",
                    "Add the drained hominy and the shredded turkey breast and simmer another 20 minutes until everything has taken on the red broth. Stir in the splash of white vinegar to brighten it.",
                    "Shred the cabbage and chop the cilantro into separate containers, cut the lime, and toast or crisp the tortillas to serve alongside."
                ],
